      "writtenOn": "2016-11-17T22:33:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I wonder if we should do a little more validation, before returning the results. Specifically... should we ensure that ie_length of each |wifi_scan_result| is 0?\n\nConsider the following situation:\n- driver\u0027s wifi_get_cached_gscan_results() naively copies IE data into wifi_scan_results. (this is buggy, because the IE info would overlap the next scan result. but i\u0027d like to be robust to bugs where it\u0027s reasonably possible.)\n- upper layers use the same code to wifi_scan_result, regardless of whether the result is coming from a gscan event, or from a full result\n- in this case, upper layers will provide garbage IEs, and also overrun the buffer\n\nWe could avoid this by checking that all the |ie_length| fields are zero here. If a non-zero value is found, either a) zero it, and log a message, or b) abort. (I\u0027d lean towards zero-and-log, since I think that\u0027ll fixup the data in any common buggy case. But maybe I\u0027m not being imaginative enough, about likely bugs.)\n\n(Sorry I didn\u0027t mention this in the previous revision!)",
      "revId": "d8caeb14e3c13392a7aca434be5489b19bfe0fd0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d0bc8cd0_5fab1ebe",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/default/wifi_legacy_hal.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 391,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074459
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-11-17T23:30:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm. We could do this. But, I\u0027m worried if there is going to be a performance impact with this loop. \nIn the conversion functions that I had written up (yet to upload), I was explicitly using a flag |is_ie_data_present| to indicate if I need to parse out the IE info from the scan result or not.\nFor the |onResults| callback, the flag is set to false and for the |onFullResult| callback the flag is set to true.\n\nSo, we\u0027ll end up having 2 loops around all these scan results one in WifiLegacyHal and one in WifiStaIface to convert to HIDL type from legacy HAL type.",
      "parentUuid": "d0bc8cd0_f6bdfe66",
      "revId": "d8caeb14e3c13392a7aca434be5489b19bfe0fd0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "30040898_89f4bd65",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/default/wifi_legacy_hal.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 391,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-11-18T20:04:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we need to be super-concerned about iterating over the data twice. It\u0027ll probably all be in L2 cache anyway. (And checking the value of |ie_length| should be highly predictable.)\n\nI see your point about the |is_ie_data_present| flag. That does make the code _within_ the upper layer clear. But IMO, the interface _between_ the layers is a bit subtle with the current CL. And the |is_ie_data_present| flag in the upper layer wouldn\u0027t make the cross-layer interaction simpler.\n\nThat said, I\u0027ll leave the performance-vs-robustness trade-off to your judgment.",
      "parentUuid": "d0bc8cd0_5fab1ebe",
